 "rulebook": "Official Baseball Rules (MLB, 2024 edition): 2.01 and Appendices 1-2 (the pitcher's plate 60 ft 6 in from the back point of home plate, 10 in above it; the mound), 2.02 (home plate), 3.01 (the ball), 5.07(a) (the pivot foot in contact with the plate), 5.07(c) (the 15 s pitch timer), Definitions of Terms: \"strike zone\", \"in flight\"",
 "deviations": [
  "3.01 (the ball in the pitcher's hand): the hand is a two-jaw gripper, Unitree's Dex1-1 (90 mm stroke, 80 mm jaws, at most 120 N of clamp, opening at most 375 mm/s; Unitree sells it for the G1, the H2 and the R1, not the H1-2: here it sits on the H1-2's right wrist where the palm ends); the ball starts held between its jaws by their friction only (no fingers, no seams: no grip-made spin beyond what the jaws' motion gives). The release is the moment the ball last touched you before it had touched nothing of you for 0.02 s.",
  "Definitions (strike zone): there is no batter, so the zone's height is fixed, 0.46 m to 1.07 m above home plate (the hollow of the knee to the midpoint of the torso of a 1.8 m batter). A strike is called when the ball's centre crosses the zone's front plane (home plate's front edge) inside the zone, in flight; the rule's \"any part of the ball through any part of the zone\" (the plate's whole depth, the ball's edge) is not used: stricter.",
  "5.07(a): \"the pivot foot in contact with the plate\" is judged at the release: a foot that touches the mound and whose sole is over the plate; either foot may be the pivot foot. Wind-up and set positions and balks are not judged (no runners).",
  "5.07(c): the pitch timer runs from the start of the episode: the ball must be released within 15.0 s.",
  "No batter, catcher or umpire: a backstop behind home plate; one pitch (a ball dropped or caught again is no second chance), and a fall before the call ends the attempt as a failure.",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model: drag and Magnus lift), still air."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Pitch a strike: from the pitcher's plate on the mound, with a foot on the plate, throw the baseball held in your right gripper through the strike zone over home plate, on the fly.",
  "rules": "The baseball (0.073 m across, 0.145 kg, it flies in air with drag and spin lift) starts held between the two jaws of your right hand, a two-jaw gripper that holds only by friction: the ball can slip or roll out and drop. You stand on the pitcher's plate (a slab 0.61 m wide and 0.152 m deep, level with the mound's top) on a mound 5.486 m across whose top is 0.254 m above home plate; in front of the plate the mound falls 1 in 12. The front edge of the pitcher's plate is 18.44 m from the back point of home plate; home plate is 0.432 m wide and as deep, so its front edge is that much nearer. The strike zone is the rectangle above home plate's front edge, 0.432 m wide and from 0.46 m to 1.07 m above home plate. The pitch: the release is the moment the ball last touched you, once it has then touched nothing of you for 0.02 s (until then it is still in your hand, wherever it rests on you). There is one release: within 15.0 s of the start, and at that moment at least one of your feet is in contact with the pitcher's plate (it touches the mound and part of its sole is over the plate). After the release no part of you may touch the ball and your gripper may not hold it again (both jaws on it), except that your right arm and its gripper may still brush it during the first 0.2 s. You succeed when the ball's centre then crosses the plane of the zone inside the zone, before the ball has touched the ground. You fail if no foot is on the plate at the release; if the ball is not released in time; if it touches the ground (the mound included) at any time before home plate, also before the release; if it crosses the plane outside the zone (a ball); if it is held again or touched after the release (but for the grace above); if you fall before the call (your pelvis lower than 0.75 m above home plate's level, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground or the mound); or after 20 s.",
  "venue": "You stand on the mound facing home plate (+x), both feet across the pitcher's plate, your pelvis above its middle; home plate's front edge is 18.1 m ahead and 0.254 m below the plate you stand on. The mound's level top reaches 0.15 m in front of the pitcher's plate, 0.56 m behind it and 0.76 m to each side. A backstop stands 3 m behind home plate. Your right forearm is level and points forwards, the gripper's fingers forwards and its jaws closed on the ball (they close along the palm's normal, sideways: one jaw on each side of the ball). Still air. The jaws open when your right grip command goes to 0 (at most 375 mm/s).",
  "difficulty": "From 18.44 m the ball needs more than 13 m/s even on a high arc, and the zone is 0.43 m wide: a release direction right to about one degree. The H1-2 has no throwing motion; the speed has to come from the trunk and the whole arm while a foot stays on the plate and the body on the mound, and two jaws that open no faster than 375 mm/s have to let the ball go at the right hundredth of a second."
 },
